 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering time is proportional to the number of active bricks $k$ and the resolution of each brick. The dynamic update loop adds overhead for pointer reassignment and texture updates, but this is constant per frame if $k$ and $m$ are fixed [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is $O(k \cdot m \cdot W \cdot H)$, where $W$ and $H$ are the spatial dimensions of the frames. This is independent of the total number of frames $N$, allowing scalability to arbitrarily large video datasets [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Enables interactive rendering of video volumes that exceed GPU memory. Supports multi-field visualization (volume + flow geometry) within the same bricking framework [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Limited to temporal partitioning; not suitable for spatially large volumes with few frames. Requires pre-computation of optical flow and seed points [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Ideal for large video datasets (e.g., time-varying scalar fields) where the spatial resolution is manageable but the temporal depth is not. [S1, unofficial]
-*   **When not to use:** Not suitable for static 3D volumes or datasets where spatial partitioning is more appropriate. [S1, unofficial]
